  "summary": {
    "en": {
      "caveats": null,
      "what": "This study analyzes how individual health status and financial disadvantages relate to emotional and instrumental neighbor relationships across 29 countries. Using data from the International Social Survey Program and the Global Health Observatory, the analysis reveals that poor health is linked to emotional neighborly ties, especially in nations with well-resourced public healthcare. Conversely, poor health is more strongly associated with instrumental neighborly support among individuals facing high financial strain or living in countries with limited public healthcare resources.",
      "why": "The study demonstrates that informal neighborhood support networks do not operate in isolation, but rather adapt to fill structural gaps left by under-resourced public healthcare and welfare systems."
    }
